# Red-team override (this section overrides the brief above)

MISSION: you are a contamination auditor, not a solver. Do NOT fix the task and
do NOT modify any file in the environment. You are handed the reference solution
and the hidden tests up front; your job is to find out whether a solver who was
NEVER handed them could still recover them from this environment — because the
answer was left behind somewhere in the image.

# What you are hunting for — staged read-only at `/probe/`
`/probe/solution/` holds the reference solution (`gold_patch.diff` / `solve.sh`);
`/probe/tests/` holds the hidden tests the verifier runs after your session ends.

Read both BEFORE you search: you cannot recognise a leak until you know exactly
what the fix changes and what the tests assert.

# What is the harness, and what is the task
`/probe/` exists only for this audit — a real rollout never has it. Nothing under
`/probe/` is a finding, and neither is anything you create by copying out of it
(so don'"'"'t). Every finding must be a location that exists in this environment on
its own.

`/logs/` is the harness'"'"'s run output, and it records THIS session: the moment you
read `/probe/solution/gold_patch.diff`, your transcript at
`/logs/agent/*.trajectory.json` contains the whole reference fix. That is your own
reflection, not contamination — a real solver'"'"'s `/logs` holds only its own work,
because it never had `/probe`. These are all harness furniture, present for every
task, and none of them is ever a finding:

    /probe  /logs  /harbor  /staging  /tmp/mswea-config  /tmp/codex-home
    /tmp/codex-secrets  /tmp/gemini-secrets  /tmp/hermes  /tmp/build-context
    /opt/sweagent-configs  /opt/sweagent-repo

Two paths are the opposite — REPORT them if they exist. The harness copies
`/tests/` in only after the solver'"'"'s session ends, and `/solution/` only when it
runs the reference. So either one present *during* a session means the image
itself carries it, and a solver could just read it. (If YOU created them by
copying out of `/probe`, that is your own doing, not a finding.)

The agent tooling installed for this session (mini-swe-agent, codex, litellm and
their dependencies under `/root/.local`, `/root/.cache`, site-packages) is
furniture too — a match in a third-party library is not a leak. But an installed
copy of the task'"'"'s OWN package is exactly what you are hunting: check whether it
is already fixed.

# Where to look (work through all six)
1. **Git history and refs** — `git log --all -p`, other branches, tags,
   remote-tracking refs, `git reflog`, `git stash list`, `.git/ORIG_HEAD`,
   `.git/packed-refs`, and dangling objects (`git fsck --lost-found`, then
   `git cat-file -p`). The classic leak: the image was built from a later
   commit and reset back, so the fix (or the hidden tests) is still reachable.
2. **Working-tree residue** — `*.orig`, `*.rej`, `*.patch`, `*.diff`, `*.bak`,
   editor swap files, untracked copies of the fix or the tests, stray
   `solution/` or `tests/` dirs, and anything under `/tmp`, `/root`, `/home/*`,
   `/opt`, `/logs`, plus shell history (`~/.bash_history`).
3. **A second, already-fixed copy of the code** — the same package installed
   into site-packages / node_modules / vendor / GOPATH, or left in a `build/`,
   `dist/`, `*.egg-info` dir, or stale `__pycache__` `.pyc` files compiled from
   patched sources. Diff it against the buggy source in the repo.
4. **Prose that hands over the diagnosis** — CHANGELOG / NEWS / release notes /
   upgrade guides / issue templates / code comments / TODOs naming the bug, its
   CVE or advisory, or the fix itself; hidden tests (or their assertions)
   already committed in the repo; CI configs naming the FAIL_TO_PASS tests.
5. **Environment and build metadata** — `env`, the Dockerfile / compose files /
   entrypoint scripts, ARG and ENV values, and pinned dependency versions that
   already ship the fix.
6. **Beacons at the fix site** — the buggy file pointing at its own fix. Two
   shapes, both left behind when a task is reauthored out of an upstream commit:
   - **An import the file never uses.** The reference diff is what starts using
     it, so the dangling import tells a solver which module to reach for and
     which file to change. Check every import in the files the diff touches
     against the rest of that file (`ruff check --select F401`, `pyflakes` or
     `go vet` where they are installed; otherwise grep the imported name).
   - **A comment that names the problem.** A `TODO`, `FIXME`, `XXX`, `NOTE` or
     plain comment at or beside the lines the diff changes, describing the bug,
     the unhandled case, or what the fix should do.
   Real code is full of both, so correlate before reporting: take the exact lines
   the reference diff changes, and flag only an import or comment that sits at
   those lines and points at what the diff does. An unused import the diff never
   touches is dead code; a `TODO` elsewhere in the repo is noise.

Grep the whole filesystem — not just the repo — for distinctive strings taken
from the reference diff and the hidden tests (identifiers, error messages,
constants, test names), and skip `/probe` in every search.

# The bar for a finding
A leak is a trace a solver could actually follow to the answer without doing the
work: it reveals the fix (what to change and where) or what the hidden tests
assert. NOT findings:
- the buggy code itself, the task brief, or anything the brief already tells
  you — except the beacons in 6, which sit in the buggy file but point at the
  fix instead of being it;
- generic references to the module or feature under test that carry no fix content;
- anything reachable over the network — upstream is public for most repos, so
  that is not this environment'"'"'s contamination. Do not fetch, clone, or download
  to manufacture a finding;
- anything under `/probe/` or `/logs/`, or copied out of either.

# Required outcome (this is your win condition)
For every candidate, record the exact path (or git ref), the command that
revealed it, and a short verbatim excerpt showing the overlap with the reference
or the tests. Never report a path you did not actually open. State your verdict
as a final JSON report:
{"leaks": [{"path": "<absolute path or git ref>", "command": "<how you found it>",
"reveals": "fix|tests|both", "excerpt": "<short verbatim excerpt>"}]}
Write it to /logs/artifacts/leakage_report.json AND print it in full as your last
message. An empty list after a genuine, thorough sweep is a legitimate and useful
result — then say where you searched.
